# PROVENANCE: measured, not generated. Every response byte below was captured
# from a genuine DESFire EV1 8K over PC/SC by a PC/SC capture tool
# and is used here as an expectation for our emulator. As with the other
# hardware_genuine_*.vec files, this vector is the opposite of the rest
# of the corpus: the others were recorded from the reference engine, so they
# pin what we decided; this one pins what the silicon does. A mismatch here is
# either a real divergence or a difference we can justify in writing, never a
# vector to be re-recorded from our engine.
#
# Card: UID 04304622216A80, hardware 1.0, software 1.4, storage 8K. Formatted
# with FormatPICC under the all-zero PICC test key, then provisioned with
# a synthetic structure whose every key is a published test key. That is what
# makes an authenticated capture safe to commit and replayable: no key material
# enters the tree, and our engine derives the same session key from the same
# pinned nonces.
#
# RndA is the fixed constant the recorder uses; RndB is the card's, recovered
# by decrypting its first authentication frame with the test key and
# pinned here with inject-random so our engine offers the same challenge.
# What this vector is for: GetFreeMemory after each allocation, measured:
#   after FormatPICC                    7936 octets
#   after one two-key application       7808 octets
#   after a 32-octet file               7744 octets
#   after a 1-octet file                7712 octets
#   after a 33-octet file               7616 octets
#   after a fourteen-key application    7328 octets
#   after DeleteFile                    7328 octets
#   after DeleteApplication             7328 octets
# Each reading carries a tolerance of 512 octets: the specification leaves
# memory accounting to the chip, so a replay checks that an engine is
# close, and a reading outside the tolerance is a real divergence.

name: genuine EV1 8K, free memory as applications and files come and go
origin: physical-card
uid: 04304622216A80
picc-key-settings-1: 0F
picc-key-settings-2: 01
picc-auth-mode: D40
picc-key-len: 16
picc-generation: EV1
picc-storage: 8192

scan
# Free memory after FormatPICC.
exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => 001F009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7936 octets free after FormatPICC; memory accounting is the chip's own)
#
# An application with two DES keys.
status   90CA000005A3A2A10F0200 => 00
exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => 801E009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7808 octets free after one two-key application; memory accounting is the chip's own)
status   905A000003A3A2A100 => 00
#
# A 32-octet Standard Data file.
status   90CD0000070100EEEE20000000 => 00
exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => 401E009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7744 octets free after a 32-octet file; memory accounting is the chip's own)
#
# A 1-octet Standard Data file.
status   90CD0000070200EEEE01000000 => 00
exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => 201E009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7712 octets free after a 1-octet file; memory accounting is the chip's own)
#
# A 33-octet Standard Data file.
status   90CD0000070300EEEE21000000 => 00
exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => C01D009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7616 octets free after a 33-octet file; memory accounting is the chip's own)
#
# OPEN DIVERGENCE, card versus engine: how free memory is accounted.
#
# Our engine reports the card's storage less the declared size of every
# data and record file. The card also charges each application for its
# directory entry and keys - 128 octets for two DES keys, 288 for fourteen
# AES keys here - and gives nothing back on DeleteFile or DeleteApplication
# before the next FormatPICC. The readings above stay within the tolerance;
# from the fourteen-key application on, the difference is more than a
# chip's layout explains, so the steps below are recorded unexecuted.
# Restore them when the engine accounts for application and key overhead
# and keeps deleted space allocated until a format.
#
#
# An application with fourteen AES keys, created from the PICC level.
#   exchange native-apdu 905A00000300000000 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 90CA000005B3B2B10F8E00 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => A01C009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7328 octets free after a fourteen-key application; memory accounting is the chip's own)
#
# DeleteFile of the 1-octet file, which KS1 0F lets anyone do.
#   exchange native-apdu 905A000003A3A2A100 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 90DF0000010200 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => A01C009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7328 octets free after DeleteFile; memory accounting is the chip's own)
#
# DeleteApplication of the AES application from the PICC with no session.
#   exchange native-apdu 905A00000300000000 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 90DA000003B3B2B100 => 91AE
# DeleteApplication without a session is refused with AE.
#
# The same DeleteApplication under the application's own master key.
#   exchange native-apdu 905A000003B3B2B100 => 9100
#   inject-random 628D5A92AE4C1706CC7AA332F1ADE2C3
#   exchange native-apdu 90AA0000010000 => 6EE90F4DE43DDD8159BB2E52C782D49791AF
#   exchange native-apdu 90AF000020ACD1F820E895AA8E9522E9948C3CF5EC1817F0ABC279DA203B9D60635A2C202800 => BCD041C4676AFB8742FD68C5D855A78D9100
#   exchange native-apdu 90DA000003B3B2B100 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 905A00000300000000 => 9100
#   exchange native-apdu 906E000000 => A01C009100 tolerance 0+3 512
#   (tolerance above: 7328 octets free after DeleteApplication; memory accounting is the chip's own)
